            COEXISTENCE TOOLS FOR OpenVMS TO BE PACKAGED
                WITH DIGITAL'S UNIX OPERATING SYSTEM


  ... Six tools from leading vendors included with latest operating
                  system software distribution ...


MAYNARD, Mass. -- September 13, 1994 -- Digital Equipment 
Corporation today announced that six key tools for interoperability 
and coexistence with OpenVMS computing environments will be included 
at no charge with its latest version of the DEC OSF/1 operating 
system.  DEC OSF/1 software is Digital's standards-compliant 
implementation of the UNIX operating system.  The suite of 
interoperability tools are supplied under a contract between 
Digital, Sector 7, and Boston Business Computing, Ltd., Inc. 

     "Customers are implementing client server computing 
environments on the UNIX operating system," said John O'Keefe, vice 
president, UNIX Systems Marketing. "It is important to them to 
maintain both user and application consistency with the systems and 
software they have in use today. The tools included in this 
arrangement allow users to see exactly the same interface they have 
been using with their current applications, and developers can work 
with familiar programming interfaces and editors." 

     Customers who have made a commitment to UNIX face a significant
challenge in training users and application developers in their
organizations.  The tools included on this new compact disk make
editing, mail, command line, and backup functions identical between
UNIX systems and existing Digital systems running the OpenVMS
operating system.  Users, programmers, and administrators working in
mixed environments are presented with a consistent environment. 

     "Tens of thousands of users are familiar with EDT and the Mail 
utility on systems running the OpenVMS operating system," said David 
Pikcilingis, President of Boston Business Computing, Ltd.  "EDT+ and 
Vmail are two of the products we have included on this CD which 
provide users with the same editing and mail features they have on 
their OpenVMS systems. For these users, using applications on 
Digital's UNIX systems does not require learning anything new." 

     "With VX/DCL, users and developers have the same command line 
interface on systems running the DEC OSF/1 operating system as they 
have with those running the OpenVMS software," said Jon Power, 
President of Sector 7.  "It is easy to move from one system to the 
other, and to create environments in which users access applications 
which run on multiple servers and multiple operating systems."

     In addition, Sector 7 is including VX/JSP, a job and print
queue spooler which provides features identical to those found on 
OpenVMS systems.  BBC has included VCL, a DCL command line emulator, 
and Vbackup, a utility for backing up data and application files 
while users are active on the system.  In addition to providing a 
full system backup utility, Vbackup can exchange savesets with 
OpenVMS Systems.  Data backed up by OpenVMS Systems can be restored 
by Vbackup on systems running the DEC OSF/1 operating system.

     "Customers are building more mixed environments today than ever 
before," said O'Keefe.  "These tools, now included with our 
operating  system at no charge, help them manage the integration 
more easily and to maintain the investment hardware, software, 
applications and training that they have built over time."

     Boston Business Computing, a pioneer in OpenVMS emulation
software development, has been providing software to OpenVMS users 
who are coexisting with or moving to UNIX systems. During the past 
ten years, Boston Business Computing has provided its software world 
wide as well consulting services for companies considering 
integrating their OpenVMS environment with other open systems.

     Sector 7 is a world wide leader in application migration from 
the OpenVMS and PDP-11 environments to UNIX systems with offices in 
the USA and Europe. Many of the worlds leading OpenVMS software 
developers have benefited from the ability to migrate applications 
to UNIX systems effortlessly using tools and services provided by 
Sector 7.  Known collectively as VX/TOOLS these packages include 
over 300 OpenVMS system services for DEC OSF/1 including RMS, SMG, 
and FMS. All VX products provide an OpenVMS call compatible 
interface for FORTRAN, C, COBOL and VX/BASIC, Sector 7's VAX BASIC 
to C transpiler.

