        ALPHA AXP 64-BIT RISC DEPLOYMENT IN EMBEDDED/REALTIME
       SECTOR WILL EXPAND VIA INTEGRATION OF CROSS DEVELOPMENT
                TOOL SUITE FROM GREEN HILLS SOFTWARE

 
   ...Suite Of Cross Development Tools Will Support Multiple Host
     Systems, Including Alpha AXP, Sun, Hewlett-Packard, Silicon
    Graphics Workstations And Intel PCs, Developing Applications
             To Alpha AXP 64-Bit RISC Target Systems...


CRYSTAL CITY, Virginia -- September 20, 1994 -- Increasing the 
momentum of Alpha AXP 64-bit RISC migration in the embedded/realtime 
Original Equipment (OEM) market, Digital Equipment Corporation today 
announced the support of a cross development environment that will 
greatly facilitate the development of realtime and embedded 
applications from multiple host systems to Alpha target systems.  
Green Hills Software, the industry's leading provider of cross 
compiler development tools, will develop their suite of compiler 
solutions and MULTI Development Environment to target Digital's 
Alpha AXPvme line of PCI-based VME single board computers.  The 
announcement was made at the Air Traffic Controllers Conference, 
which opened here today at the Hyatt Regency Crystal City Convention 
Center.

      Through this development agreement, Santa Barbara, CA-based 
Green Hills Software will provide a fully integrated development 
environment for Alpha AXP in realtime and embedded applications. 
The Green Hills suite of cross compiler solutions will provide 
integrated C and C++ languages supported by sophisticated debugging 
and programming tools, and is also designed to fully leverage the 
rich embedded application development environment afforded by the 
VxWorks for Alpha AXP software.  OEM customers may purchase the 
integrated Green Hills/Alpha AXP solutions directly from the Green 
Hills sales channel.  

     "Within two years, we expect that most realtime and embedded 
OEMs will be developing applications on Alpha AXP host systems," 
said Steve Hargrave, of Digital's Embedded and Realtime Products 
Group.  "The availability of Green Hills' solutions will allow these 
OEMs to develop higher-performance, 64-bit RISC applications for 
Alpha AXP targets using their existing development environments.  
This is an important step in realizing our long-term objective for 
Alpha in the embedded sector."

     Furthermore, said Hargrave, OEMs using the VxWorks for Alpha 
AXP embedded kernel as well as those developing their own kernel 
services will be able to take advantage of the Green Hills 
capabilities.  "The combination of the VxWorks for Alpha tools with 
the Green Hills integrated development environment significantly 
enhances the attractiveness of the Alpha platform to OEMs," he said.

     "As embedded applications move towards high-performance 
RISC-based VMEbus and PCI (Peripheral Component Interface) 
solutions, OEMs are eager to capitalize on the fast performance and 
emerging standards available in Alpha AXP," said Paul Zorfass, an 
industry analyst covering the embedded and realtime market for 
International Data Corporation, a Framingham, MA-based market 
research firm.  With the integration of the Green Hills compiler 
solutions, said Zorfass, barriers to Alpha AXP migration have been 
removed for OEMs concerned about preserving existing application 
development environments and avoiding excessive migration costs.

     Hargrave stated that the Green Hills solution was the natural 
choice for Digital for two primary reasons: 1) Green Hills' 
outstanding name recognition and marketshare in the high-performance 
embedded systems market, and; 2) the capabilities of Green Hills' 
MULTI Development Environment provided superior cross-integration in 
a mixed platform environment.

     "With the MULTI Development Environment, our Alpha AXP OEMs can 
also develop applications for Motorola 68000 targets on their legacy 
Sun or Hewlett-Packard workstations," said Hargrave.  "As embedded 
OEMs juggle old and next-generation systems within a complex 
development environment, they must down load applications to a 
variety of target systems as well as handle debugging in a mixed 
computing environment."    

     Through the development agreement with Digital, Green Hills 
will have access to worldwide OEM customers, including Raytheon, 
Bailey and Phillips, as well as gain a headstart in the burgeoning 
high-performance RISC VMEbus and PCI market, according to Hargrave.  

     "Our partnership with Digital will enable Green Hills to widen 
our penetration of the emerging OEM embedded industry," said David 
Chandler, vice president of sales for Green Hills Software.  "As the 
first vendor to ship commercially-available PCI-based embedded/
realtime solutions, Digital will provide an entree for our solutions 
in new, dynamic embedded applications areas," he said.

     "Furthermore, our development agreement with Digital will 
showcase the capabilities of our tools in a whole new performance 
realm afforded by the 64-bit RISC Alpha AXP architecture.  We are 
very proud to offer tools for the fastest chip in the world," said 
Chandler.

Pricing And Availability

     The integrated Green Hills/Alpha AXP solution will be available 
during the second quarter of 1995.  Prices begin at $5,400.  For 
more information on the solutions, please call  1-800-AXP-4-VME or 
David Chandler, vice president of sales for Green Hills Software at 
805-965-6044.   						    

     Incorporated in 1982, Green Hills Software is recognized 
worldwide for having the best optimizing compilers for 32-bit and 
64-bit microprocessors in the industry.  Each Green Hills compiler 
utilizes more than 100 optimizations to produce highly-efficient 
code for optimal application performance.  The MULTI Software 
Development Environment includes a source level debugger that 
supports debugging in C, C++, FORTRAN, Pascal, Ada and assembly.  
MULTI also includes a program builder, source code version control 
system, editor, graphical browser, execution profiler, coverage 
analyzer and run-time error detector.

     Green Hills products are available on all popular UNIX 
workstations, including Alpha AXP/OSF 1, DECstation/ULTRIX, Sun 
SPARC (SunOS and Solaris 2.x), HP 9000/700, IBM RS/6000, Silicon 
Graphics workstations, a Windows 3.1.  Green Hills products are 
distributed to North America, South America, and Europe through 
OASYS, One Cranberry Hill, Lexington, MA  02173, (617) 862-2002, and 
through Advanced Data Controls Corporation (ADaC), (03) 3576-5351, 
for Japan. 

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha AXP 
platforms, storage, networking, software and services, together 
with industry-focused solutions from business partners, help 
organizations compete and win in today's global marketplace.
