             DIGITAL ANNOUNCES THE VME UNIX SUPERSERVER
 

     ...Specially-Designed to Meet the Needs of VME Users in the
     Telecommunications, Image Processing and Aerospace/Defense
  Industries, the 2100 VME Superserver Satisfies the Most Demanding
           High-Performance, Large Memory Applications...


CRYSTAL CITY, Virginia -- September 20, 1994 -- Digital Equipment 
Corporation today announced the industry's first-to-market 
superserver that offers both VME and PCI technology together.  An 
extension of the company's highly-successful Digital 2100 Server 
Model A500MP released in April, 1994, the new RISC-based UNIX SMP 
server based on the Alpha AXP architecture combines the Digital 2100 
server series' core 64-bit Alpha and PCI technology with VME 
extensions in an integrated industrial rackmount design to meet the 
mechanical packaging requirements of OEMs.  The announcement was 
made at the Air Traffic Controllers Conference, which opened here 
today at the Hyatt Regency Crystal City Convention Center.

     Developed by Digital's Embedded and Realtime Products Group, 
the new server based on the Alpha AXP architecture provides the 
ideal bridge to next-generation bus architectures for OEMs in the 
embedded and realtime market sectors -- primarily in the aerospace/
defense, image processing and communications industries.  
Entry-priced at $31,500, the server unites Digital's strongest core 
technologies -- the power and speed for Alpha AXP Symmetric 
Multiprocessing (SMP); the high performance of the PCI bus; and the 
versatility of the VME64 bus -- in an integrated product providing a 
built-in migration path to future technologies.

     According to Bill Armitage, Vice President of Digital's 
Embedded and Realtime Products Group, "The Digital 2100 shattered 
the computer industry's traditional expectations of price/
performance in the VME server market.  With this new server, we 
have extended the high-performance Alpha AXP technology to appeal 
to the needs of the OEM community, by adding flexible packaging 
alternatives, and -- most importantly -- the industry-standard
VME bus.

     "Digital has consistently linked the importance of the PCI 
standard to the long-term strength of the VME market," Armitage 
continued.  "Because we are the first-to-market with a powerful 
VME/PCI product, Digital is the leader in enhancing VME systems 
with PCI technology." 

Market Trends Drive Demand for New Server

     Frank Casagni, Segment Manager for Defense and Aerospace in 
Digital's Embedded and Realtime Products Group, noted that the 
server's introduction, which marks the first high-end RISC-based 
SMP/VME solutions of its kind, will provide greater opportunities to 
develop powerful VME RISC and high availability UNIX solutions for 
the embedded and realtime market sectors, particularly in high-
performance application areas.  "VME is moving to the high end; 
advanced 64-bit RISC VME now has the greatest market potential," he 
said. "And realtime UNIX SMP is critical to the marketplace's compute-
intensive embedded applications, such as image processing, 
simulation and signal processing.

     "The performance of SMP versus multiple Single Board Computers 
on VME can be dramatic," Casagni continued.  "Interprocess 
communication speeds can be one hundred times faster.  High 
performance UNIX SMP servers complement multiple Single Board 
Computers.  Also, users are looking for a smooth migration path, 
and a bridge to PCI bus architectures."

New Server Meets the Needs of Embedded & Realtime Users

     The new server addresses the following user needs:  1) robust, 
industry-standard UNIX with IEEE realtime extensions for embedded 
applications; 2) both VME and PCI slots for flexible I/O solutions; 
3) industry-leading SMP performance, with a built-in upgrade path; 
and, 4) vertical (fixed) and horizontal (drawer) industrial 
packaging options, to provide easier access and/or a smaller system 
"footprint."

Server Outscores the Competition in Price/Performance and 
Robustness of VME/PCI Options

     Equipped with four CPUs, the new server provides 640 SPECfp, 
and Symmetric Multiprocessing across a 667 MB/sec private CPU-memory 
backplane.  Additionally, very large physical memories are available 
-- up to 2,048 MBytes -- for applications such as realtime database 
development.  Licensing for DEC OSF/1 Version 3.0 or OpenVMS AXP 
Version 6.1, including realtime extensions, is standard in the base 
system.

     The server contains three PCI-32 slots, and five 6U slots on 
the VME64 motherboard.  An optional PCI to VME adapter can expand the 
VME I/O beyond these standard five 6U slots. The adapter provides a 
software-transparent link to an additional VME-32 chassis.

     "Digital has made intelligent tradeoffs in its latest Server 
based on the Alpha AXP technology," said Warren Andrews, industry 
analyst and Editor-in-Chief of The Real Times Magazine, "in 
optimizing its CPU/memory 128-bit, 667 MBytes/sec memory/CPU bus for 
performance, while keeping faith with the open-standard I/O 
community by providing VME and PCI capability.  This flexible I/O 
architecture," he continued, "assures the users that applications 
will not run short of I/O bandwidth now -- or in the future."

     "The server is the high-end member of Digital's long-term 
VME/PCI product strategy," Frank Casagni concluded.  "By developing 
a 100% systems-compatible family of products, from Single Board 
Computers to servers, Digital has dedicated itself to high-powered, 
open standards solutions for the marketplace."

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha AXP 
platforms, storage, networking, software and services, together 
with industry-focused solutions from business partners, help 
organizations compete and win in today's global marketplace.
